{
  "abstract": "We construct three pairwise non-isomorphic directed strongly regular graphs with parameters $(102,16,6,5,2)$, so both $(102,16,6,5,2)$ and its complement $(102,85,75,70,75)$ exist. Both rows were open. All three graphs have trivial automorphism group, consistent with paper 117, which excludes an automorphism of order 17. $(102,16,6,5,2)$ is the $q=6$ member of the line $L(q)=(q(3q-1),3q-2,q,q-1,2)$. The graphs come from a group-free tabu search seeded with a structure that I found by mining 64 unseeded graphs at $q=4,5$. In that structure the mutual part $A\\circ A^{\\mathsf T}$ has a $K_{q+1}$ component $K$. Its out- and in-shadows consist of $(q+1)(q-1)$ vertices each, each joined to exactly two vertices of $K$. The remaining vertices split into $3q+1$ mutual $K_{q-1}$ cliques. Freezing this structure raises the hit rate at $(70,13,5,4,2)$ from 2/68 to 19/30 restarts, and gives three hits at $q=6$ within 7 restarts on three of four seeds. Unseeded search there never got below cost 384. A pure-Python verifier checks the dsrg identity for each graph and its complement; nauty gives six distinct certificates for the graphs and their converses; one graph is reproduced byte for byte from its seed. The structure is an observation, not a proved consequence of the parameters.",
